WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:10.629 --> 00:00:22.199
This 
paper does a number of things.

00:00:22.199 --> 00:00:29.140
It starts by reminding the Board of the nature
of Specialised Services, and then goes on

00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:37.360
to propose some principles and a method by
which we can prioritise the introduction of

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.100
new developments and new interventions.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:48.000
Specialised Services is a very, very complex
agenda because these are treatments for the

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.309
rarest disorders.

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:58.640
They are often for patients who are extremely
ill, and the treatments themselves are often

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:02.360
complex and expensive.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:08.380
Often, we find ourselves in a situation where
it is difficult to use the tried and trusted

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:16.090
methods of identifying what works really well
because of the rarity of the disease and the

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:20.210
inability to do large trials.

00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:25.880
What we need is a method so that we can identify,
when new treatments and new developments come

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:31.619
along, how NHS England can decide which of
those to prioritise, which of those to provide

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:35.860
for patients.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.729
The first part of the paper reminds us of
the extent of Specialised Commissioning and

00:01:39.729 --> 00:01:44.389
that there is a £14 billion budget there.

00:01:44.389 --> 00:01:50.149
I probably would want to say that the majority
of treatments that NHS England commissions

00:01:50.149 --> 00:01:56.770
out of the £14 billion budget are tried and
tested, and we are absolutely clear that they

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:02.399
have considerable impact for patients and
that they deliver value for money for the

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:03.399
NHS.

00:02:03.399 --> 00:02:09.759
What we are looking for now is, how do we
define a decision making process that allows

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:16.410
us, in an open, transparent and timely way,
to decide when there may be additional things

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:22.030
that we need to add to the list of things
that we routinely commission.

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:27.360
I would also like to make the point that this
is about adding to the list of things that

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.080
we routinely commission, or identifying the
range of things that we routinely commission.

00:02:33.080 --> 00:02:38.440
It does not preclude the payment for a treatment
for an individual patient.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:45.100
At NHS England, we have to make a decision
as to those types of care, which we say to

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:50.900
doctors and clinicians in the front line,
‘This is routine care in NHS England.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:55.430
If you initiate this treatment for a patient,
it will be funded, it will be paid for, it

00:02:55.430 --> 00:02:56.500
will be available’.

00:02:56.500 --> 00:03:03.040
However, there are always exceptions; there
are times when it is clear that something

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.080
is not necessarily appropriate for a group
of people, but for one or two individuals

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:09.890
with their specific circumstances.

00:03:09.890 --> 00:03:16.380
Then, it is actually really important that
we can look at an individual patient and decide

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:20.330
whether, in that particular instance, this
might be ideal for the patient.

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:24.510
So, just to make very clear: this is about
what we would routinely commission.

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:29.480
It is not about individual funding requests,
which still remain in place, and it is not

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:34.260
about the Cancer Drugs Fund, which is not
a part of this process.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:40.430
If we look at the background, we have in place
a set of transitional arrangements.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:47.070
NHS England is slightly older than it was,
but we are still a relatively new organisation,

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:53.130
and we inherited a lot of different ways of
working from all the PCTs.

00:03:53.130 --> 00:04:00.180
We put in place a set of transitional arrangements,
which we agreed that we would then work with

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:05.370
stakeholders, patients, the public, all those
who could and should have a voice, to put

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:10.040
this on a firmer and more long term footing,
and that is where we find ourselves at the

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.430
moment.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:15.930
I think this is particularly apposite at the
moment, again, given the conversation we had

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:21.560
about allocations and the clarity that the
Specialised Commissioning budget has a significant

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:27.130
increase in it for next year, and therefore,
our ability to look at new treatments and

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:35.840
interventions is more important now that that
increase in funding is available.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:42.200
Over the past few months, we have been working
with a number of stakeholders in order to

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:48.420
bring to the Board a set of proposals on which
we can then go out to wider consultation.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:52.270
The things in the paper you see before us
have not been widely consulted, and that is

00:04:52.270 --> 00:04:58.320
precisely what we are proposing, but we have
worked with significant numbers of patients,

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:05.550
other stakeholders and patients’ representatives
in order to get to this point.

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:11.470
What we are proposing to talk to you today
about and ask the Board for agreement for

00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:18.260
consultation is the two key elements of the
principles of the decision making; in other

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:24.020
words, exactly what, at all stages of decision
making, is the comprehensive set of principles

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:28.430
that everyone in NHS England will adhere to
through our process.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:32.300
I think, to be honest, those principles are
self explanatory, so I will not go through

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.290
those in detail.

00:05:34.290 --> 00:05:40.050
The second thing that we are wanting to talk
about is the characteristics of the process

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:46.350
through which we will make those decisions,
and I will talk a little bit more about that.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:50.910
Again, if I can remind the Board – although
this was mentioned earlier – that there

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:55.500
are certain things over which we do not have
a choice; whatever our funding allocation

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:03.210
is, we have to do what we have called first
order interventions, such as NICE Technology

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:04.210
Appraisals.

00:06:04.210 --> 00:06:05.430
There is a second order.

00:06:05.430 --> 00:06:10.550
In exactly the same way we would with any
other budget, we have to deliver the 18 week

00:06:10.550 --> 00:06:16.861
standards, the cancer standards for Specialised
Services in the same way as we expect the

00:06:16.861 --> 00:06:23.220
CCGs to do so, and therefore, this would be
an absolute second order call on the allocation

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:25.750
for Specialised.

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:31.370
What we are calling the third order is where
we have collectively agreed a clear strategy,

00:06:31.370 --> 00:06:36.460
and the developments clearly fit into the
strategy and direction of travel to which

00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:37.460
we are all committed.

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.710
There are a couple of examples there.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:46.160
Then the fourth order is the considerable
range of other Specialised Services developments

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:47.910
that we receive.

00:06:47.910 --> 00:06:55.100
I think it is really important to remember
that we have duties to many people in NHS

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:56.100
England.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:00.720
We have absolute duties to patients to make
sure that those treatments that make a difference,

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.520
that really do work and will improve their
life expectancy or the quality of their lives,

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:08.630
are there and available for them.

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:13.440
We also have a duty to the taxpayer and the
nation to make sure that every penny we have

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:19.540
is spent wisely, and that we make the most
difference to the greatest number of patients

00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:21.949
that we possibly can.

00:07:21.949 --> 00:07:26.710
Because of the complexity and the exceptional
cost that is sometimes involved in Specialised

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:31.190
Commissioning, I think nowhere is it more
stark than that there are some difficult choices

00:07:31.190 --> 00:07:32.220
to be made.

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:41.180
What we are proposing in terms of how we do
this, which we believe will be a much more

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:48.620
structured process than we have had in the
past, is outlined in section 15.

00:07:48.620 --> 00:07:54.430
There are some quite fancy names in these,
but I will try to explain them as clearly

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:55.430
as I can.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:59.270
The environmental scanning phase is really
about our Clinical Reference Groups looking

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:02.280
around and identifying all the things that
might be coming through.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.670
One of the things we need to do about this
process is get more onto the front foot and

00:08:06.670 --> 00:08:11.479
be able to deliver a continuous process, not
have a situation where we have a one off opportunity

00:08:11.479 --> 00:08:17.290
for people to make proposals to us once a
year; we want to turn this much more into

00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:19.520
a continuous process.

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:25.330
The Clinical Reference Groups will be looking
to identify what is coming through, but I

00:08:25.330 --> 00:08:32.520
think we could do a bit more work on exactly
what happens with those individuals proposals,

00:08:32.520 --> 00:08:35.970
because we have found in the past that some
proposals have come a long way through the

00:08:35.970 --> 00:08:39.690
system, and when you look, it is clear that
they are funded in a routine way, through

00:08:39.690 --> 00:08:41.539
tariff or somewhere else in the system.

00:08:41.539 --> 00:08:45.720
That environmental scanning is not only about
identifying what is available, but identifying,

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:50.600
‘Is this treatment or development right
to carry on through this process?’

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:56.420
There will be a plethora, a huge range of
things come up through the Clinical Reference

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:57.420
Groups.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.600
I think the next phase will be for the National
Programmes of Care, which are more groupings

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.420
of the Clinical Reference Groups, to start
to look at those and give us some sort of

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:11.600
order out of them, so some sort of prioritisation
as to which ones fit with the broader strategies,

00:09:11.600 --> 00:09:17.000
which ones it is that we should be really
moving on to look at in great detail.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:22.870
The clinical build phase is about the detailed
work that makes sure we understand there is

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:28.980
a really strong evidence base behind the proposal,
to be absolutely certain that by the time

00:09:28.980 --> 00:09:33.750
we get to a point where we are making decisions
there is no question about what evidence there

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:34.750
is.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:40.880
Bear in mind what I said earlier, that sometimes
with rare disorders, evidence is not as readily

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:46.810
available as with some, but nonetheless, that
is going to be so vitally important.

00:09:46.810 --> 00:09:52.670
Running almost concurrently with that is understanding
the financials, particularly the impact analysis,

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:56.550
but also, all the other things that we do
with impact analysis about reducing inequalities

00:09:56.550 --> 00:10:02.310
so that we get to a point when the Clinical
Priorities Advisory Group is coming together

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:10.180
to advise the newly formed Specialised Commissioning
Committee on behalf of the Board that it has

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:17.620
every piece of information it needs in order
to make its decisions and make recommendations

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:20.430
as to what we should do next.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:25.640
There is a lot behind those phases which is
not in this paper.

00:10:25.640 --> 00:10:31.240
The detail behind those phases will be in
the consultation process.

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:35.290
One of the things I think it is important
to be clear about is that each of those phases

00:10:35.290 --> 00:10:39.740
will have a decision making point in them,
so that the only decision making is not when

00:10:39.740 --> 00:10:44.100
the final decision is made in the governance,
but actually, each of those phases will have

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:48.400
a decision making point, and therefore, we
need to be really clear with patients, the

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:52.880
public and all stakeholders where those decision
points are, how they can be involved, how

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:58.459
they can influence, and how they can shape
the process so that they are involved in that.

00:10:58.459 --> 00:11:03.200
Those are the two things that we would propose
to consult on.

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:08.800
I think that it is worth saying to the Board
that one dilemma for us is probably the length

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:13.950
of this consultation, and it would be really
helpful to me if I could have a steer on how

00:11:13.950 --> 00:11:16.020
long we should consult for.

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:22.910
On the one hand, I think there is a strong
argument with something as complex as this

00:11:22.910 --> 00:11:27.060
and such a big decision for us that a 90 day
consultation – the more traditional three

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:31.250
month consultation – is more meaningful
and really does give patients, the public

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:37.209
and all stakeholders the chance to collect,
collate and give their views in a meaningful

00:11:37.209 --> 00:11:38.910
way.

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:44.770
The downside to that is that we have been
going through this process for a while, and

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:49.660
we have a number of treatments waiting in
the wings ready to be assessed that I think

00:11:49.660 --> 00:11:54.430
a lot of stakeholders would have a reasonable
expectation that we would be assessing in

00:11:54.430 --> 00:11:59.290
March, and clearly a 90 day consultation with
the process then following would probably

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:03.170
push those out into some time in the summer.

00:12:03.170 --> 00:12:07.770
Two things I would say to the Board in terms
of mitigation against that is that, firstly,

00:12:07.770 --> 00:12:12.360
there is no change to our ability to deal
with urgent issues where something comes along

00:12:12.360 --> 00:12:17.230
where it is clearly going to make a very significant
difference to a cohort of patients and it

00:12:17.230 --> 00:12:20.750
has to be initiated urgently because of their
condition.

00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:24.120
We have routine processes for that, and that
would continue.

00:12:24.120 --> 00:12:28.850
Secondly, I would remind about the individual
funding requests, which are still there.

00:12:28.850 --> 00:12:33.370
Thirdly, because of the additional funding
going into Specialised Services, what we would

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:38.740
propose to do would be to allocate their full
budgets to the regional teams who will hold

00:12:38.740 --> 00:12:48.360
the Specialised budgets, but make it absolutely
clear that we would expect them to reserve

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:53.730
and have in their plans the volume, the financial
allocation, in order to then fund these areas

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:57.180
as they go through the process and we make
a decision.

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:02.390
We are not in a situation whereby we make
decisions in July then we have the scenario

00:13:02.390 --> 00:13:05.250
where people say, ‘But we have made our
plans and all the budget is spent’.

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:09.600
If we were to go for the 90 day consultation,
we would make sure that the holders of the

00:13:09.600 --> 00:13:17.420
budget were clear that they had to plan to
have that money available.

00:13:17.420 --> 00:13:22.660
I may mention to the Board, if I could, that
our own stakeholder group of patients have

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:26.380
indicated to us that, of the two, they would
prefer the 90 day consultation, but obviously

00:13:26.380 --> 00:13:32.080
there is a much broader range of stakeholders
as well, all of whom will have views.

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:37.150
I am happy to answer any questions I can on
this.

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:41.029
What I am asking for, really, is, do we think
the principles are right?

00:13:41.029 --> 00:13:45.709
Are we happy about the process and the stages
that we have described?

00:13:45.709 --> 00:13:50.010
Is everybody happy that the Board will approve
and we can launch the consultation in the

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:55.240
new year and delegate the returns to the Specialised
Commissioning Committee?

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:58.510
And, if you would, please, a steer on the
length of the consultation.

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:02.630
We will maybe take those points in the order
in which Barbara has put them to us.

00:14:02.630 --> 00:14:06.970
First of all, can I get a steer from the Board
as to whether they believe that launching

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:11.030
this process is the correct thing that we
should be doing?

00:14:11.030 --> 00:14:16.040
Secondly, the principles that are outlined
in the paper.

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:23.260
Thirdly, the stages of decision making, the
prioritisation criteria.

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:28.740
And fourthly, the stages, which start with
environmental scanning and go right through

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:32.579
to the final prioritisation.

00:14:32.579 --> 00:14:37.740
We are today, after all, only being invited
to approve a set of principles which will

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:41.510
then go out to consultation, and we will hear
further from that.

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:44.830
Let me then turn to the consultation question,
Barbara, because I think there is a question

00:14:44.830 --> 00:14:47.930
to ask.

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:54.910
We have done 30 day consultations in the past,
and we know that in some circumstances that

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:56.360
works perfectly well.

00:14:56.360 --> 00:15:02.280
Are you suggesting to us that 90 days is what
we are hearing from stakeholders, and, given

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:07.839
the complexity of this and the variety of
stakeholders, would have a greater likelihood

00:15:07.839 --> 00:15:15.149
of returning a richer set of information for
us, but also, that you have the capacity in

00:15:15.149 --> 00:15:23.540
the interim to continue to manage commissioning
for treatments that are urgently required

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:24.839
for patient groups?

00:15:24.839 --> 00:15:29.120
In other words, we are not delaying a whole
slew of approvals in order to go through a

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:32.500
90 day consultation.

00:15:32.500 --> 00:15:40.530
There will be some delay, because what we
have is the facility to accelerate anything

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:47.850
that is urgent, but we have known about many
of these cases for a while and they would

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:52.829
not meet those criteria, so they would need
to go through the full process.

00:15:52.829 --> 00:15:59.350
We have to balance the risk that some of those
will wait another two or three months before

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:04.470
there is a clear decision against – as you
rightly point out, Chairman – the richness

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:08.100
that we would get back from a consultation.

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:13.380
One of the issues, of course, is that with
such rare disorders, people have them all

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:22.320
over the country, and it is always more difficult
to consult with families of patients with

00:16:22.320 --> 00:16:25.660
rare disorders simply because they have more
difficult in having a presence.

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:31.339
That is why every individual CCG cannot consult
on this, because there are not enough people.

00:16:31.339 --> 00:16:36.160
Therefore, quite a lot of the really important
stakeholders here will need to consult across

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:44.970
the country with a variety of people.

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:51.709
It is only our own patient group, but we have
had a letter – I think it was to Simon – from

00:16:51.709 --> 00:16:56.759
our Specialised Services Client Group – because
we have a patient group that helps and advises

00:16:56.759 --> 00:17:02.050
– saying that they would prefer it to be
three months.

00:17:02.050 --> 00:17:08.269
There is no doubt that we very frequently
do 30 day consultations, and it would be normal

00:17:08.269 --> 00:17:11.549
and perfectly appropriate to do a 30 day consultation.

00:17:11.549 --> 00:17:18.580
It would be slightly unusual for us to do
90 days on something of this nature, but I

00:17:18.580 --> 00:17:23.260
think the thoughts about it have actually
been precipitated by the patients themselves

00:17:23.260 --> 00:17:24.730
asking us to do that.

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:27.049
I think it is difficult.

00:17:27.049 --> 00:17:33.840
It is a difficult decision because there are
pros and cons to each.

00:17:33.840 --> 00:17:35.890
I think it is a very easy decision.

00:17:35.890 --> 00:17:40.799
30 days to consult an awful lot of people
and an awful lot of organisations on something

00:17:40.799 --> 00:17:47.240
of this magnitude and complexity just does
not work, and I think Specialised Commissioning

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:52.120
has in the past produced some major consultations
and expected people to drop everything over

00:17:52.120 --> 00:17:54.120
Christmas and get back in 30 days.

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:59.870
It just would not work, so I think it is entirely
straightforward, and it is 90 days, then we

00:17:59.870 --> 00:18:02.080
live with the consequences.

00:18:02.080 --> 00:18:12.700
Because of the nature of Specialised Commissioning
and, as Ciarán says, the complexity of the

00:18:12.700 --> 00:18:18.320
areas with which one is potentially dealing,
it strikes me that, as a statutory body, we

00:18:18.320 --> 00:18:23.860
have a duty to have regard to everything that
is relevant and we put ourselves in a difficult

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:31.440
situation if we have too narrow a period,
because we will not engage with the widest

00:18:31.440 --> 00:18:36.080
possible number of potential stakeholders.

00:18:36.080 --> 00:18:42.960
I would err, as Ciarán would, on the side
of the 90 days, in order that we can have

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:49.550
regard to as much as we can, so that we are
not in danger of inadvertently disregarding

00:18:49.550 --> 00:18:57.870
something that we ought really to have had
paid relevant attention to.

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:05.669
I would suggest 90 days, and in particular,
I feel more confident in that because we do

00:19:05.669 --> 00:19:14.770
have the ability to deal with urgent issues,
so if anything does suddenly need urgent attention,

00:19:14.770 --> 00:19:22.940
we can accelerate that, so it is not as though
we would be leaving an area in jeopardy by

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:28.290
lengthening the period of consultation.

00:19:28.290 --> 00:19:30.090
I agree.

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:37.150
I think on this occasion we should exercise
our discretion to have a more extended consultation

00:19:37.150 --> 00:19:39.910
period than might normally be required.

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:46.820
Just so that the Board is clear on the point,
legal advice has been that a 30 day consultation

00:19:46.820 --> 00:19:53.070
on those topics would normally be perfectly
appropriate, but we obviously have discretion,

00:19:53.070 --> 00:20:00.900
and given the range of considerations that
this much broader paper has, I think we should

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:02.690
exercise our discretion.

00:20:02.690 --> 00:20:11.960
Under the circumstances, and given the real,
serious character of these proposals and their

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:19.260
complexity, I detect that the Board would
prefer to go for a 90 day consultation, against

00:20:19.260 --> 00:20:24.080
the assurances that Barbara has been able
to give us about our flexibility to respond

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:27.170
to urgent cases.

00:20:27.170 --> 00:20:36.240
I think that I have agreement around the Board
to the principles, the process and the consultation.

00:20:36.240 --> 00:20:40.660
Just before you conclude, I have just been
thinking about the infrastructure that makes

00:20:40.660 --> 00:20:47.830
this complex and highly engaging process more
effective.

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:54.420
I think the NHS Citizens process, going forward,
should be part of this.

00:20:54.420 --> 00:21:00.380
I am concerned whether we have the resources
to do so.

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:04.230
I am a bit worried about the cuts that we
made earlier and the impact.

00:21:04.230 --> 00:21:09.030
On the one hand, we have to engage with the
communities around complex issues.

00:21:09.030 --> 00:21:15.540
We have a framework as a means of doing that,
and the first meeting of the project that

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:24.590
we had a few days ago has some kick off; we
are going to get off to a strong start.

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:28.710
On the other hand, we may be losing the resource
that enables us to do this kind of thing effectively

00:21:28.710 --> 00:21:33.250
and as a matter of course, which, by the way,
will involve quite a significant shift in

00:21:33.250 --> 00:21:37.130
the skills of our staff in the Operations
department, going forward.

00:21:37.130 --> 00:21:41.620
I am just a bit concerned that we skate over
that fact.

00:21:41.620 --> 00:21:45.900
It is quite a significant culture shift and
operational shift.

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:49.200
I am just going to say two things on that.

00:21:49.200 --> 00:21:56.000
One is that we have a process between January
and March for agreeing our business plan for

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:57.000
next year.

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:01.419
We have to publish that for ‘15/’16, and
that is the process where we have to make

00:22:01.419 --> 00:22:05.130
these internal resource decisions, so that
is a decision that the Board has to hammer

00:22:05.130 --> 00:22:06.130
through.

00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:13.310
Secondly, as part of the £200 million for
transformation, we are obviously going to

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:18.080
expect that local areas working with us are
going to use some of that to resource these

00:22:18.080 --> 00:22:20.020
kinds of processes as well.

00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:24.770
I think we have to think about both aspects
of that, but the underlying point you make

00:22:24.770 --> 00:22:25.770
is obviously right.

00:22:25.770 --> 00:22:33.390
Just to add to that, I think, going back to
the guidance, we have now signalled that this

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:38.350
very important structural priority around
properly prioritising the patients as the

00:22:38.350 --> 00:22:43.320
voice of decisions we make is for real, and
against that, commissions must be judged.

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:47.470
That is obviously an issue in terms of programme
budget allocation, which Simon says we will

00:22:47.470 --> 00:22:51.070
deal with, but I think one of the lessons
that we need to recognise is that what we

00:22:51.070 --> 00:22:55.540
are being taught her about the process of
Specialised Commissioning over the last couple

00:22:55.540 --> 00:23:01.169
of years is that we have not successfully
deeply embedded the patient voice in the decision

00:23:01.169 --> 00:23:05.090
making process, and this is an opportunity
for us to not only consult on the priorities,

00:23:05.090 --> 00:23:08.580
but also start to think and challenge – as
I know you will do in the Task and Finish

00:23:08.580 --> 00:23:12.780
Group – ourselves around the kind of culture
we actually need to start promoting properly

00:23:12.780 --> 00:23:15.230
as an organisation.

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:16.320
Okay.

00:23:16.320 --> 00:23:20.370
I had not anticipated that this would be a
process simply of publishing a paper and sitting

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:22.539
back and waiting for responses to come in.

00:23:22.539 --> 00:23:26.660
I think within the time and the resources
that we have, we need to take on board Victor’s

00:23:26.660 --> 00:23:32.430
and Tim’s comments and work out how we get
a proper engagement, a proper triangulation,

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:37.510
of views that can bring a richer intelligence
to us in the process.

00:23:37.510 --> 00:23:43.570
I absolutely agree on that, but it will need
a kind of matrix organisation.

00:23:43.570 --> 00:23:49.510
The Specialised Commissioning team is very
small, and we need the support from particularly

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:53.590
Tim’s department, the directors and the
clinical directors, because that is where

00:23:53.590 --> 00:23:57.870
the expertise comes from in terms of working
with the public.

00:23:57.870 --> 00:23:59.720
Thank you very much.

00:23:59.720 --> 00:24:03.409
I have one final request of the Board on this
paper, which is, will you authorise the Chief

00:24:03.409 --> 00:24:07.190
Executive in consultation with me to sign
off the consultation paper?

00:24:07.190 --> 00:24:09.630
Can I just make a point on the principles?

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:18.010
I think if we are going through this process,
we should have a bit of a look at CRGs themselves.

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:20.929
Are they constituted correctly?

00:24:20.929 --> 00:24:24.390
Do they have this process of engaging people
who look at things through multiple lenses?

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:30.169
There is another question around whether we
have the right CRGs, because one of the risks

00:24:30.169 --> 00:24:35.429
of being quite specialised is they will judge
things in terms of being specialised as opposed

00:24:35.429 --> 00:24:39.960
to, actually, the really effective intervention
is non specialised and it is prevention, public

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:42.000
health, or something else.

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:46.670
I think if we are going to look at this, let
us maybe look at it a bit more broadly there

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:49.130
as well, and take the pathway view.

00:24:49.130 --> 00:24:52.730
Even though it is specialised, it is difficult,
it is complicated, and it is small numbers

00:24:52.730 --> 00:25:00.049
of people, etc, maybe some of the interventions
would benefit from people with different expertise,

00:25:00.049 --> 00:25:04.190
or wider expertise might be a better way of
saying it.

00:25:04.190 --> 00:25:09.250
If that could be in the loop as well, then
I think our 90 days will be, again, more than

00:25:09.250 --> 00:25:10.250
justified.

00:25:10.250 --> 00:25:15.960
It is such a good point, and one that will
continue in development after the 90 days

00:25:15.960 --> 00:25:19.440
as we get the new structures into place.

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:21.860
Can I just check everyone is happy to delegate
as proposed?

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:26.730
The future oversight of the process will go
to the Specialised Commissioning Committee.

00:25:26.730 --> 00:25:33.200
What I think we ought to do, though, is bring
back the report from the consultation, which

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:38.010
reflects on how that has worked, what is the
richness of opinion that it has delivered

00:25:38.010 --> 00:25:44.540
to us, then we will do the further delegations
as a Specialised Commissioning committee.

00:25:44.540 --> 00:25:45.540
Thank you very much.

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:52.580
Let me move then to item 10 on the agenda,
which is the three reports that I propose

00:25:52.580 --> 00:25:53.650
to take as read.

00:25:53.650 --> 00:25:58.470
They are starred; nobody has asked for them
to be un-starred, so starred they will remain.

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:01.409
That leads me finally to any other business.

00:26:01.409 --> 00:26:07.850
Firstly, the date of the next meeting is 29th
January, to be held here in London.

00:26:07.850 --> 00:26:15.330
Secondly, I think all of us on the Board wish
to take this opportunity to commend those

00:26:15.330 --> 00:26:19.120
who deliver frontline services across the
NHS.

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:21.660
We have heard a lot about delivery today.

00:26:21.660 --> 00:26:27.169
I do not think any of us would underestimate
the enormous strides the NHS is making all

00:26:27.169 --> 00:26:33.750
the time to meet sharply rising demand for
its services, and to do so at world class

00:26:33.750 --> 00:26:40.429
levels of quality and clinical safety, and
at world class levels of low investment.

00:26:40.429 --> 00:26:46.570
I particularly think it is worthwhile on this
occasion commending those who provide excellence,

00:26:46.570 --> 00:26:51.930
those who provide leadership, both in the
clinical setting, in management, in CCGs and

00:26:51.930 --> 00:26:56.330
right across the piece, because those are
the teams we will be working with as we come

00:26:56.330 --> 00:27:00.330
through the next year in the implementation
of the forward view.

00:27:00.330 --> 00:27:06.630
Can I also, on behalf of the non executive
members of the Board, express our thanks and

00:27:06.630 --> 00:27:08.960
congratulations to the executive team?

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:16.570
Today has been a very commanding, challenging
set of items that we are being asked to consider,

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:21.419
and I do know personally just how much time
and work has gone into all of this.

00:27:21.419 --> 00:27:25.590
It brings us back to a question which we will
have to continue to keep under review, which

00:27:25.590 --> 00:27:33.160
is our shared capacity, to continue to take
a lead across England in the transformation

00:27:33.160 --> 00:27:40.140
that is proposed in the forward view, and
the Board is conscious of the burden that

00:27:40.140 --> 00:27:43.440
we are imposing upon the national directors
and the rest of our team.

00:27:43.440 --> 00:27:49.429
May I simply conclude by saying thank you
to everybody, and wishing everybody a restful

00:27:49.429 --> 00:27:56.790
– except for those on duty over Christmas
and the new year – season, and we look forward

00:27:56.790 --> 00:28:00.029
to picking up the battle again at the beginning
of January.

00:28:00.029 --> 00:28:01.029
Thank you very much.

00:28:01.029 --> 00:28:01.270
The meeting is closed.

